    "title": "Brand Architecture Systems: House of Brands vs Branded House",
    "module": "Brand Architecture & Market Positioning",
    "estimatedMinutes": 25,
    "content": "<h2>Brand Architecture Is a Business Decision</h2>\n<p>How a company organizes its brands \u2014 branded house (all products carry the parent brand: Apple iPhone, Apple Watch) vs. house of brands (each product is independent: P&G's Tide, Pampers, Crest) \u2014 is one of the highest-leverage decisions a CMO makes. Get it right and you have a scalable system that amplifies every product launch. Get it wrong and you spend millions maintaining brand confusion that undermines both parent equity and product growth.</p>\n<h2>Branded House Architecture</h2>\n<p>Every product carries the parent brand. Advantages: new products launch with instant credibility from the parent; brand investment compounds across all products; customers know who they're dealing with. Risks: a product failure damages the parent brand; the parent brand constrains positioning for products that serve different segments; one identity must serve all customers.</p>\n<h2>House of Brands Architecture</h2>\n<p>Each product is a separate brand. Advantages: products can be positioned independently for different segments; failures are isolated; acquisition brands maintain their equity. Costs: each brand requires independent investment; parent brand builds no direct consumer equity; organizational complexity scales with brand count.</p>\n<h2>Endorsed Brand Architecture</h2>\n<p>The optimal model for most B2B companies: a master brand with product sub-brands that carry the parent name. Salesforce Sales Cloud, Salesforce Marketing Cloud, Salesforce Service Cloud. This captures credibility transfer of a branded house while allowing product differentiation. The CMO's job is maintaining the master brand equity that makes endorsement valuable.</p>\n<h2>When to Change Architecture</h2>\n<p>Architecture changes are triggered by: entering an adjacent market where the parent brand is a liability, M&A bringing a strong independent brand, or portfolio expansion creating too much complexity. The decision test: does the proposed product benefit more from parent brand association than from independent positioning?</p>",
