    "estimatedMinutes": 25,
    "content": "<h2>Two Types of Competitive Strategy</h2>\n<p>Every company makes one of two choices: competing within an existing category (trying to win market share from established players) or designing a new category (defining the problem space so your solution is the only logical answer). These require different marketing approaches, different investments, and different metrics.</p>\n<h2>Competing for Market Share</h2>\n<p>Competing in an established category is a feature-benefit battle: who has better capabilities, lower prices, or stronger relationships. Marketing is comparative positioning \u2014 where you win against specific alternatives, reaching buyers in the decision phase. This is defensible but exhausting: you're one feature release away from losing your advantage. Your competitor's roadmap determines your relevance.</p>\n<h2>Owning a Category</h2>\n<p>Category ownership means you define the problem, own the language, and position yourself as the architect of a new way of thinking. Salesforce didn't build better CRM \u2014 they declared the death of software and created \"No Software.\" HubSpot didn't build marketing tools \u2014 they defined \"inbound marketing\" and made outbound the villain. Category kings capture 76% of category value. This is a structural competitive advantage, not a marketing tactic.</p>\n<h2>The Positioning Statement Framework</h2>\n<p>Answer four questions: <em>For whom?</em> (specific buyer profile). <em>With what problem?</em> (specific pain you solve). <em>What solution?</em> (your specific approach). <em>Why different?</em> (the unlike anything else statement). \"For B2B sales teams who waste 40% of rep time on unqualified leads, our AI qualification platform continuously learns from outcomes to deliver pre-qualified pipeline \u2014 unlike static lead scoring that requires manual tuning.\"</p>\n<h2>The Three Positioning Tests</h2>\n<p><strong>Mirror test:</strong> Could a competitor claim these exact words? If yes, you're describing the category, not your position. <strong>Care test:</strong> Does the target audience genuinely care about the problem you're solving enough to pay a premium to solve it? <strong>Evidence test:</strong> Do you have proof \u2014 case studies, data, references \u2014 that you deliver the outcome you claim?</p>",
    "keyConcepts": [
        "Category Ownership vs Market Share",
        "Positioning Statement Framework",
        "The Three Positioning Tests",
        "Category Value Distribution",
        "Language Ownership Strategy"
    ],
