    "content": "<h2>MMM Is Back \u2014 More Important Than Ever</h2>\n<p>Marketing Mix Modeling experienced a dramatic renaissance. The death of third-party cookies, iOS privacy restrictions, and fragmented cross-channel journeys make individual-level tracking increasingly impossible. MMM \u2014 using aggregate data without individual tracking \u2014 is now the most durable approach to understanding marketing's revenue contribution at the macro level.</p>\n<h2>How MMM Works</h2>\n<p>MMM uses multivariate regression to decompose revenue into components: base sales (without marketing), each channel's contribution, and external factors (seasonality, economic conditions). By varying historical spend across channels, the model estimates each channel's response function \u2014 its contribution at different spend levels. This reveals not just what a channel contributed but how its contribution changes at different investment levels.</p>\n<h2>What CMOs Must Understand</h2>\n<p>You don't need to build the model. You need to: understand data inputs required (typically 2-3 years of weekly spend and revenue by channel), interpret the output (response curves, saturation points, ROI by channel), and apply findings to budget allocation. The model is a tool; business judgment about how to use it is your job.</p>\n<h2>MMM Limitations to Acknowledge</h2>\n<p>MMM is a lagging indicator \u2014 models trained on historical data may not capture rapidly changing channel dynamics. It requires significant historical data (2+ years). It operates at an aggregation level that can't optimize individual campaigns. Use MMM for strategic budget allocation while using real-time channel data for tactical optimization.</p>",
    "keyConcepts": [
        "MMM Statistical Foundation",
        "Revenue Decomposition",
        "Response Curves and Saturation",
        "Data Requirements",
        "MMM vs MTA Complementarity"
    ],
