    "content": "<h2>Marketing Owns the Demand Signal</h2>\n<p>Marketing sees demand earlier than any other function. Website traffic, content engagement, demo requests, trial starts, and intent data are all observable before a buyer enters the sales pipeline. The CMO who builds predictive revenue models from these leading indicators can tell the CEO what pipeline will look like in 60-90 days \u2014 not just what it looks like today. This elevates the CMO to the center of revenue planning.</p>\n<h2>The Leading Indicator Hierarchy</h2>\n<p>Signals have different lead times: website traffic and content engagement (60-90 day lead), form fills and content downloads (45-60 day lead), demo requests and trial starts (30-45 day lead), and sales-accepted leads (15-30 day lead to opportunity). Each predicts future pipeline at different time horizons, and the relationship between signal volume and pipeline can be modeled historically.</p>\n<h2>Building the Predictive Model</h2>\n<p>Step 1: identify your most reliable leading indicators (signals most correlated with eventual pipeline). Step 2: measure conversion rates and time lags between each signal and pipeline creation. Step 3: model: current signal volume \u00d7 historical conversion rate \u00d7 time lag = projected pipeline. Step 4: validate against 6-12 months of historical data before using for forward projections.</p>\n<h2>Presenting the Forecast</h2>\n<p>\"Current leading indicators project $2.8M in new pipeline over 45 days, range of $2.2M\u2013$3.4M. The key risk is that demo request volume is 15% below this week's historical average, which could reduce the projection to the lower bound if the trend continues.\" This is CFO-quality forward communication applied to marketing intelligence.</p>",
